    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras are positioned for a high-ceiling offensive output today, anchored by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.98) and **Trea Turner** (79.40), who emerge as our premier plays. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from an elite matchup against Randy Dobnak, buoyed by a 14% platoon advantage and favorable wind conditions that lift his profile into the elite tier. Similarly, **Trea Turner** remains a centerpiece of the lineup; despite Shane Bieber\u2019s pedigree, his 5.23 xERA suggests vulnerability, and the combination of **Trea Turner**\u2019s elite speed and a significant 19% SP Skill boost makes him a vital catalyst for our SB and R targets. **Kyle Schwarber** (78.21) joins them as a top-tier option, benefiting from the same exploitable matchup against Bieber and a substantial +10% order bonus from his leadoff spot.\n\nOur bench logic was refined by tight margins, with **Lars Nootbaar** (46.60) serving as the most difficult omission. While **Lars Nootbaar** posted a respectable score against Justin Wrobleski, the optimizer ultimately pivoted to **Carson Benge** (47.49) and **Kazuma Okamoto** (46.99) due to superior platoon splits and batting order security. The lineup remains heavily invested in **Yordan Alvarez** (65.05), who anchors our middle order despite the lack of a defined opponent, while we continue to monitor the status of **Dillon Dingler** (40.09) and **Junior Caminero** (60.45); both carry the inherent risk of the \"Lineup Pending\" designation, though **Junior Caminero** remains a solid play given his strong xwOBA. We are proceeding without **Casey Schmitt**, who remains sidelined on the IL, while keeping depth options like **Josh Smith**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** ready should late-breaking lineup changes force a rotation in the bottom third of our slots.",
